  "status": "published",
  "title": "Huawei Convicted on 10 Counts. Jury Acquits It of Racketeering.",
  "deck": "A Brooklyn jury found the Chinese telecom giant guilty of bank fraud, sanctions violations and conspiring to steal trade secrets. Huawei says it plans to appeal.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T21:41:44.179Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T21:41:44.179Z",
  "body": [
    "A federal jury in Brooklyn found Huawei Technologies guilty on 10 of 12 criminal counts Tuesday, including charges tied to its business in Iran and a conspiracy to steal trade secrets from U.S. companies. Jurors acquitted Huawei of racketeering conspiracy and conspiracy to obstruct justice.",
    "The verdict followed a 12-week trial that also yielded convictions against three Huawei subsidiaries, including Huawei Device USA, based in Plano, Texas. Prosecutors had argued that the company used a pattern of criminal conduct to build its global telecom business. The split verdict leaves Huawei facing punishment for specific offenses while clearing it of the government's broader racketeering charge.",
    "What prosecutors brought to trial",
    "A central issue in the fraud case was Skycom, the company Huawei used to do business in Iran. Federal prosecutors said Huawei described Skycom to banks as a business partner when it was actually a Huawei subsidiary. According to the Justice Department, those misrepresentations helped Huawei retain access to loans, credit and the banking services it needed to move U.S. dollars.",
    "The trade-secret case reached into American telecom labs. Prosecutors told jurors that Huawei employees sent photographs and technical details of a T-Mobile robot used to test phone touchscreens to colleagues in China. The Justice Department said an employee also removed a part of the robot from a T-Mobile lab. The government cited copied software code from Cisco routers as another example.",
    "Huawei's lawyers argued that prosecutors stitched together isolated incidents to make a case about how the whole company operated. Huawei said the trade-secret disputes had previously been resolved in civil litigation. Its lawyer, Brian Heberlig, welcomed the racketeering and obstruction acquittals and said the company expected to appeal the convictions.",
    "What happens next",
    "The jury's guilty verdicts establish criminal liability on the counts it upheld. The racketeering acquittal leaves the government's broader claim of a criminal enterprise unproven. The split verdict alone does not reveal how jurors weighed the evidence on that charge.",
    "U.S. District Judge Ann Donnelly is scheduled to sentence Huawei on September 14, 2027, Reuters reported. The Justice Department says the defendants face possible fines, forfeiture and restitution. The court has yet to determine how much they will have to pay."
  ],
